{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And he placed it on the lobe of Aaron's right ear and on the thumb of his right hand and on the big toe of his right foot.</b> It appears to me regarding the arrangement of these limbs that since all of existence is divided into three parts — the world of the angels, the world of the spheres, and the lower world — and Moses was commanded concerning the Tabernacle, which encompasses the parts of this existence: inside the curtain corresponding to the world of the angels, outside the curtain which is the Tent of Meeting corresponding to the world of the spheres, the courtyard of the Tabernacle corresponding to the lower world — therefore this Tabernacle required that there be there one who recognizes his Creator, uniquely set apart to serve the service of the Holy One, blessed be He, and that he also encompass in his body and in the configuration of his form these three parts. And this is the form of man, who is a microcosm: in him is the world of speech, which is the head, corresponding to the world of the angels; and the world of the living — this is from the chest to the waist, for through the intermediary of his body is the heart, which is the possessor of motion, corresponding to the world of the spheres; and the world of nature from his waist downward corresponding to the lower world. And since the sacrifices are the sustenance of the world, which is divided into these three parts, Moses our master, peace be upon him, needed to place some of the blood — which is the atonement of the soul — on these three limbs of the body, which are the three parts of the body of the priest who makes atonement. And this is what it says: 'on the lobe of Aaron's right ear' — this is the world of speech, for the organ of the ear is in the head; 'and on the thumb of his right hand' — this is the middle of the body, which is the world of the living; 'and on the big toe of his right foot' — this is the world of nature." "<b>And know</b> that the thumb, which is the language of the Torah, is called in the language of the Sages 'godel,' which is the largest of them all. And there are those who explained that the largest of them all is called 'zeret,' and the smallest of them all is called 'godel'; and thus the arrangement of the five fingers of the hand by their names is: etzba, amah, kemitzah, godel, zeret. The first, which is the largest, is called 'zeret' because through it the span is completed when one measures in spans; the second is called 'etzba' — and this is 'and the priest shall dip his finger in the blood'; the third is called 'amah' and it is the middle one, longer than them all, and the cubit of the arm is completed through it; the fourth is called 'kemitzah' — and this is (Leviticus 2) 'and he shall take a handful from there, the fullness of his fist'; the fifth is called 'godel' and it is the smallest of them all, and it is the end of the fingers, and therefore it is called 'godel' from the language of 'gadilot' (fringes), which are threads at the edge of the garment." "<b>However,</b> at the beginning of Ketubot (5a) they said: this is the etzba, this is the zeret, this is the kemitzah, this is the amah, this is the godel. And the explanation of Rashi, of blessed memory, there: 'this is zeret' — with the small one they measure the span of the breastplate. 'This is kemitzah' — from the finger next to the small one they begin to grasp, covering three of his fingers — the kemitzah, the amah, and the etzba — and grasp. 'This is amah' — large, the cubit of building and the cubit of vessels. 'This is etzba' — for sprinkling, as it is written 'with his finger.' 'This is godel' — for the thumb of Aaron's hand. And each one of them has its need, even for the needs of Heaven' — thus far the words of the Rav, of blessed memory." "<b>And the natural philosophers</b> wrote in their books that just as each and every limb of the body's limbs was created for a specific benefit in the body, and in most of them each one comprises two or three benefits and there is nothing in vain, so too the five fingers of the hand — each and every one of them was created for a specific benefit. And this is that each of the fingers serves one sense of the five senses in man, which are the essence of man, and through them the powers of the soul are manifest. The first serves to wipe the mouth, and in it is the sense of taste; the second within the nostrils, in which is the sense of smell; the third for the sense of touch, to feel in all parts of the body, and therefore it is longer than them all; the fourth to wipe the eye; and the fifth to wipe the ear. And the Holy One, blessed be He, created five senses in the body of man and assigned them five servants, which are the fingers. And this is the perfection of nature, for nature does nothing in vain, and we see that each and every one of the fingers goes to its sense always without intention. Blessed and exalted be the Fashioner, the Fashioner of beginnings, who fashioned man with wisdom."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:24:15.313603428Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}